============================================
(a) jobs.py thread-safety  [mcp_server/jobs.py]
============================================
- All mutations under _LOCK: launch insert + Thread.start() (L42-53), _run
  status/result/error/finished writes (L73-85), prune deletions (L99-111,
  caller holds _LOCK per module docstring L18-21). get_job reads + shallow
  copy under lock (L59-61). PASS.
- No TOCTOU on record reads: get_job returns dict(record) taken atomically
  under _LOCK; _run writes status/result/finished inside one lock block, so a
  copy never observes a mid-transition status. PASS.
- Insert-before-start invariant: _JOBS[job_id] = {...} (L44) precedes
  Thread.start() (L51-53), both inside the lock. _run's first statement is the
  fn call (no lock), and its status write needs _LOCK, which launch_job holds
  until after start(). => the record ALWAYS exists before any status write;
  no window where a fast job completes before launch_job returns the id.
  (The record may legitimately already be "completed" at first poll - the
  contract only promises the id is valid, not that status==running.)
  PASS.
- Exception capture: _run wraps fn in try/except Exception (L70-79); error
  recorded under lock; threads never die silently. Note: BaseException (e.g.
  KeyboardInterrupt/SystemExit) is NOT caught - a worker raising those would
  die with no record. In a server process this is a non-issue; theoretical
  only. PASS with note.
- Daemon threads: Thread(..., daemon=True) (L52). No process-hang. PASS.

Races considered (none blocking):
  R1 (theoretical): get_job's shallow copy aliases the mutable result object
  stored in _JOBS; a hypothetical consumer mutating result would corrupt the
  registry. Current consumers only json.dumps the copy. No fix needed.
  R2 (accepted design): cap can exceed 64 while >64 jobs run concurrently
  (running never evicted); a client spamming launches spawns unbounded
  concurrent threads. Explicitly documented plan trade-off (todo 1 spec
  "NEVER evict running"; plan acceptance criterion allows transient over-cap).
  Not a deviation.
  R3 (accepted): prune() is lock-free by convention; direct calls are safe
  only when no other thread mutates _JOBS. All in-repo call sites satisfy
  this (launch_job under lock; test calls after _run threads finished or
  while the 4 running jobs are Event-blocked, i.e. not touching _LOCK).
  PASS.

============================================
(b) Eviction correctness  [jobs.py L88-112]
============================================
- TTL: drops only records with finished is not None and now-finished>JOB_TTL
  (L101). Running records (finished=None) never TTL-dropped. PASS.
- LRU cap: while len>MAX_JOBS, collects only finished records; if none, break
  (running-only, cap may transiently exceed 64 - plan-accepted); else evicts
  min by finished timestamp (L103-111). Running NEVER evicted. PASS.
- Call-site consistency: launch_job calls prune() under _LOCK (L43) matching
  the documented lock-free prune convention. PASS.

